Drives its own private GC state (its own `gcBindMemory`/`gcInit`-bound arena) and
every assertion is about that private heap: how many distinct spans the burst
touched, and whether each span's occupied-slot bitmap agrees with the
allocator's own `spanTotal`/`spanFree` counters after a churn of frees through
that private collector. None of that depends on whether the ambient (libbitrt)
collector ever runs — the private collector's objects are invisible to the
ambient one, and the collections that matter are the explicit `gcCollectRoots`
calls this program makes itself. Verified: default and `BIT_GC=stress` runs of
the built binary produce byte-identical stdout, and the default run shows zero
ambient collections (the handful of top-level arrays never cross the trigger).

#4122 added two more phases (`phaseCacheGuard`, `phaseFreeListGuard`), each its
OWN private `gcBindMemory`/`gcInit` heap, entirely independent of the one
above and of each other. Same argument applies to both: nothing they assert
depends on the ambient collector, and every object either heap allocates is on
memory the ambient collector never scans. Re-verified after adding them:
default and `BIT_GC=stress` runs still produce byte-identical stdout, and
`BIT_GC_STATS=1` still reports `collections=0` — the added top-level arrays
(a handful of bulk `[]i64`/`[]i32` allocations per phase, not a per-object
allocation for the tens of thousands of objects driven through each phase's
own PRIVATE heap) still stay under the ambient trigger.
